<p>A hailstorm crosses Hidalgo County. A pipe bursts behind a wall. A kitchen fire leaves smoke in every room. Suddenly you are dealing with repairs, paperwork, and an insurance process you have probably never been through before. At some point a question comes up: <strong>when should you hire a public adjuster?</strong></p>

<p>Timing matters more than most homeowners and business owners in McAllen realize. Claims often move quickly at the start and slow down later. Documentation has to be accurate, deadlines can pass quietly, and settlement numbers are calculated from what the insurer’s file contains. This guide covers the moments when a claim changes, the situations where you probably do not need help, and how Texas regulates public adjusters and their fees.</p>

<h2>What a public adjuster actually does</h2>

<p>Your insurance company assigns its own adjuster, either a staff employee or an independent adjuster on contract, to evaluate the claim. That adjuster works for the insurer. A public adjuster is licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) under Insurance Code Chapter 4102 and works only for the policyholder. Texas public adjuster contracts must state “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY.”</p>

<p>On a typical claim, a public adjuster will:</p>
<ul>
<li>Inspect and document the damage, including damage that is easy to miss.</li>
<li>Review your policy, endorsements, deductibles, and coverage limits.</li>
<li>Prepare a detailed, line-item repair estimate (Xactimate is commonly used).</li>
<li>Organize photos, inventories, receipts, and other proof of loss.</li>
<li>Meet the insurer’s adjuster at inspections and re-inspections.</li>
<li>Present the claim and negotiate the amount of loss with the insurer.</li>
</ul>

<p>There are also things a public adjuster cannot do. They cannot give legal advice, file a lawsuit, or take part in repairing a property on a claim they adjust. Texas conflict-of-interest rules in Chapter 4102 prohibit that. For a closer look at roles, see <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster-for-hail-claims/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster</a>.</p>

<h2>Why timing matters in an insurance claim</h2>

<p>A claim is a process, and each stage builds on the one before it. What gets photographed in the first week shapes the estimate. What goes into the estimate shapes the settlement. Gaps at the start can become costly by the end.</p>

<p>Evidence is easiest to collect right after the damage happens, before tarps, demolition, cleanup, and repairs change what can be seen. Many people wait until they feel frustrated to look for help. That is understandable, but by then the insurer’s estimate may already be built on incomplete information. For McAllen property owners, this matters most during peak hail and wind season in April and May, when insurers and contractors are handling many claims at once.</p>

<h2>Situations where you should consider hiring a public adjuster</h2>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Situations-Where-You-Should-Consider-Hiring-a-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Property owner reviewing storm damage and insurance paperwork" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Large losses, low offers, delays, and denials are common turning points in a claim.</figcaption></figure>

<h3>The damage is significant</h3>
<p>Large losses are where professional documentation usually matters most. Damage that looks like one repair can turn into several once a roof is opened or walls are removed. Common large-loss scenarios in the Rio Grande Valley (RGV) include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Roof and interior damage after hail or straight-line wind. See hail damage claims.</li>
<li>House fires and smoke damage. See <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a>.</li>
<li>Burst pipes and water damage, like the losses after the February 2021 freeze. See water damage claims.</li>
<li>Hurricane and tropical storm damage, as with Hurricane Dolly (2008) and Hurricane Hanna (2020).</li>
<li>Commercial property losses with business income questions.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The settlement offer seems too low</h3>
<p>This is the most common turning point. The insurer’s estimate comes in, and it does not match your contractor’s bid. Gaps usually come from scope (missing items or rooms), quantities, pricing, or depreciation. A public adjuster can compare the two estimates line by line and document what is missing. Our <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> page explains common causes.</p>

<h3>The claim has become complicated</h3>
<p>Some claims involve several damaged areas, multiple structures, engineers, contents inventories, additional living expenses, or business interruption. When managing the claim starts to feel like a second job, outside help can bring order to it.</p>

<h3>The claim has stalled</h3>
<p>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets timelines. Insurers generally must acknowledge a claim and begin the investigation within 15 days of notice. They then have 15 business days after receiving everything they requested to accept or reject it, with up to 45 more days if they notify you they need more time. After a declared weather-related catastrophe, these deadlines can be extended by 15 days. If your claim is sitting with no clear explanation, read <a href="/why-property-insurance-claims-are-delayed-texas/">why property insurance claims are delayed in Texas</a> and see our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a> service.</p>

<h3>The claim was denied, fully or partly</h3>
<p>A denial can feel final, but it is not always the end. Claims are denied for many reasons: not enough documentation, disagreement over the cause of damage, exclusions, or late notice. Get the denial and its reasons in writing. A public adjuster can review the letter against the policy and gather more evidence when the facts support it. See <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>. For legal questions about a denial, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>When you may not need a public adjuster</h2>

<p>Not every claim needs outside representation. Handling it yourself often makes sense when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The damage is minor and clearly covered, such as a small roof repair or a single-room leak.</li>
<li>The insurer’s estimate matches or comes close to your contractor’s bid.</li>
<li>The claim is little more than your deductible.</li>
<li>The insurer is communicating clearly and paying on time.</li>
</ul>

<p>The question to ask is whether the size or complexity of the claim justifies professional help. A free claim review can help you answer it without any obligation to hire anyone.</p>

<h2>Timing at a glance</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Claim stage</th><th>Should you consider a public adjuster?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Right after the damage</td><td>Yes, for larger or complex losses</td><td>Evidence is freshest and the claim file is still being built</td></tr>
<tr><td>Before the insurer’s inspection</td><td>Often</td><td>Helps make sure all damage is pointed out and documented</td></tr>
<tr><td>After the first estimate or offer</td><td>Frequently</td><td>A line-by-line comparison can show missing scope or pricing</td></tr>
<tr><td>When the claim is delayed</td><td>Often</td><td>Organized documentation and written follow-up can help move it forward</td></tr>
<tr><td>After a denial or partial denial</td><td>Strongly worth a review</td><td>The reasons may be answerable with more evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>After the claim is closed</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>Supplements or reopening may be possible depending on the policy and timing</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>How public adjusters strengthen a claim</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Thorough documentation:</strong> photos, measurements, moisture readings, and in some cases Matterport 3D imaging. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> shows what a complete file includes.</li>
<li><strong>Detailed estimates:</strong> line-item estimates you can compare directly with the insurer’s.</li>
<li><strong>Policy review:</strong> deductibles, replacement cost vs. actual cash value, ordinance or law coverage, and endorsements.</li>
<li><strong>Supplements:</strong> documented requests to add damage that was missed or found during repairs.</li>
<li><strong>Dispute options:</strong> when the disagreement is about the amount of loss, the policy’s appraisal clause may apply. See insurance appraisal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Understanding the cost of hiring a public adjuster in Texas</h2>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Understanding-the-Cost-of-Hiring-a-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Public adjuster contract and fee disclosure being reviewed by a homeowner" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Texas regulates public adjuster contracts and caps fees at 10% of the settlement.</figcaption></figure>

<p>Texas regulates public adjuster fees more closely than many people realize. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins, and Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Texas rule</th><th>What it means for you</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>10% fee cap (Tex. Ins. Code §4102.104)</td><td>Compensation cannot exceed 10% of the insurance settlement on the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>72-hour policy-limits rule</td><td>If the insurer pays or commits in writing to pay policy limits within 72 hours after the loss is reported, the adjuster cannot take a percentage fee (reasonable hourly compensation only)</td></tr>
<tr><td>72-hour right to rescind</td><td>You can cancel the contract within 72 hours of signing</td></tr>
<tr><td>TDI-approved contract</td><td>The contract must use approved terms and state “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Licensing</td><td>Public adjusters must hold a TDI license. Ours is #[[license]], held by [[company]].</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Before signing, ask exactly how the fee is calculated, whether it applies to amounts already paid, and what happens if you end the contract. To see how deductibles, depreciation, and fees might affect your numbers, try our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a>.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before you sign with a public adjuster</h2>

<p>If you decide outside help makes sense, take a few minutes to vet the person and the contract. A reputable public adjuster should welcome these questions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Are you licensed by TDI?</strong> You can verify a license on the Texas Department of Insurance website.</li>
<li><strong>Who will actually handle my claim?</strong> Ask who will inspect, write the estimate, and talk with the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>What experience do you have with this type of loss?</strong> Hail, fire, water, and commercial claims each have their own documentation needs.</li>
<li><strong>How is the fee calculated?</strong> Ask whether it applies to payments the insurer already made, and how it is handled if you end the contract.</li>
<li><strong>How will you keep me informed?</strong> Ask how often you will get updates and how to reach them.</li>
<li><strong>What can you not do?</strong> An honest answer includes giving legal advice, guaranteeing a result, or doing the repairs on your claim.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Red flags</span><p>Be cautious of anyone who promises a specific settlement amount, pressures you to sign the same day, offers to waive your deductible, or also wants to be the contractor on your repairs. Texas rules bar public adjusters from taking part in repairs on claims they adjust.</p></div>

<h2>Final thoughts</h2>

<p>Insurance claims can seem simple at first: report the damage, wait for the inspection, get paid. But policy language, estimates, inspections, and negotiations all affect the outcome, and small details early on can make a big difference later. For homeowners and business owners in McAllen, Hidalgo County, and the surrounding Rio Grande Valley, the best time to ask whether you need help is often at the start of the claim, not after problems appear. If you are dealing with a fire, read our guides to the <a href="/fire-insurance-adjuster/">fire insurance adjuster</a> and the <a href="/fire-insurance-public-adjuster/">fire insurance public adjuster</a>.</p>
